# ============================================================
# CURSOR RULES — NotebookLM Académie
# ============================================================
# Ce fichier est la LOI pour toute génération de code.
# Cursor DOIT respecter chaque règle sans exception.
# ============================================================

## PROJECT CONTEXT

NotebookLM Académie is a premium e-learning platform teaching people how to master
Google NotebookLM. The audience ranges from executives and business teams to
developers and students. The brand must feel: **modern, premium, intelligent, trustworthy**.

Tech stack: Next.js 14 (App Router) + TypeScript + Supabase + Tailwind CSS + Vercel.

## DESIGN IDENTITY (CRITICAL — THIS IS A PREMIUM PRODUCT)

### Visual Direction: "Refined Intelligence"
Think Apple Education meets Stripe Documentation. Clean, spacious, confident.
NOT generic SaaS. NOT template-looking. NOT "AI purple gradient" cliché.

### Typography
- Display headings: Use `font-display` class mapped to a premium sans-serif
  (e.g., "Plus Jakarta Sans", "Satoshi", "General Sans", or "Cabinet Grotesk")
- Body text: Use `font-body` mapped to a highly readable sans-serif
  (e.g., "Inter" ONLY for body, or "DM Sans")
- NEVER use default system fonts for headings
- Headings must be bold and generous in size (text-4xl to text-7xl on hero sections)
- Line heights must be generous: leading-relaxed for body, leading-tight for headings

### Color Palette (CSS variables in globals.css)
- Primary: Deep navy/indigo (#1a1a2e or similar) — trust, intelligence
- Accent: Vibrant coral/amber (#f59e0b or #ef4444) — energy, action, CTAs
- Background: Warm off-white (#fafaf8) — NOT pure white
- Surface: Soft warm gray (#f5f5f0) — cards, sections
- Text: Near-black (#111111) for headings, (#444444) for body
- Success: Emerald green for progress/completion states
- NEVER use purple gradients, bright blue #0066ff, or generic SaaS palettes

### Spatial Design
- Sections: generous vertical padding (py-20 to py-32)
- Max content width: max-w-6xl for text, max-w-7xl for full layouts
- Cards: rounded-2xl with subtle shadow-sm, hover:shadow-md transition
- Generous whitespace between elements — let the design breathe
- Use asymmetric layouts when appropriate (text left, visual right)

### Motion & Micro-interactions
- Page load: staggered fade-in animations (use CSS @keyframes + animation-delay)
- Scroll: subtle reveal on scroll using Intersection Observer
- Buttons: scale-[1.02] on hover with transition-all duration-200
- Cards: smooth hover lift with shadow transition
- Progress bars: animated fill with ease-out timing
- KEEP IT SUBTLE — premium = restrained elegance, not circus

### Imagery & Icons
- Use Lucide React icons consistently (never mix icon libraries)
- Hero sections: full-width with gradient overlays or abstract geometric patterns
- Module icons: custom colored badges, not generic clip art
- Testimonial photos: rounded-full with ring-2 ring-white shadow

### Component Patterns
- Buttons: rounded-xl, font-semibold, generous px-6 py-3 padding
- Input fields: rounded-xl, border-gray-200, focus:ring-2 focus:ring-primary
- Navigation: sticky, backdrop-blur-md bg-white/80, with smooth transitions
- Footer: comprehensive, dark background (#111111), organized in columns
- Cards: always with rounded-2xl, consistent padding p-6 or p-8

## ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLES

- Single Responsibility: one file = one responsibility
- Maximum 150 lines per file. Split if longer
- Separate: UI components / business logic / API calls / utilities / types
- Use the established folder structure. NEVER put everything in one file
- New feature = new folder with component + test + types if needed

### Route Groups (Next.js App Router)
```
src/app/
├── (marketing)/          → Public pages (landing, modules, blog, about)
│   ├── page.tsx          → Homepage / landing
│   ├── modules/          → Course catalog
│   ├── a-propos/         → About page
│   └── blog/             → Blog/articles (SEO)
├── (platform)/           → Authenticated pages (Phase 2)
│   ├── dashboard/        → User dashboard
│   └── cours/            → Course player
├── api/                  → API routes
│   ├── auth/
│   ├── newsletter/
│   └── courses/
├── layout.tsx            → Root layout
└── globals.css           → Global styles + CSS variables
```

### Component Organization
```
src/components/
├── ui/           → Primitive reusable (Button, Input, Badge, Card, Modal)
├── layout/       → Structural (Header, Footer, Container, Section)
├── marketing/    → Landing-specific (Hero, Features, Testimonials, Pricing, CTA)
├── course/       → Course-specific (ModuleCard, LessonItem, ProgressBar)
```

## CODE QUALITY

- TypeScript strict mode. No `any` types unless documented with `// WHY:`
- All functions/components: JSDoc with `@param`, `@returns`, `@example`
- Descriptive names: `getModuleById()` not `getData()`
- Functions under 30 lines. Extract if longer
- No duplication. Extract into utils/ or hooks/
- Handle ALL errors explicitly. No empty catch blocks
- Early returns to avoid nesting
- `const` over `let`. Never `var`

## SECURITY (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- NEVER hardcode API keys, secrets, passwords, tokens in any file
- ALL secrets in .env.local via environment variables
- ALWAYS maintain .env.example with placeholder values
- Validate ALL user inputs server-side with Zod
- EVERY API route: check auth + check authorization
- Parameterized queries only (no string concatenation)
- Never expose stack traces or internal errors to the client
- Never log sensitive data
- SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY: server-side ONLY

## CONTENT STRUCTURE (NotebookLM Académie specific)

The platform teaches 3 modules:
1. "L'Architecte de Données" — Foundations: RAG, sources, grounding
2. "Le Spécialiste de la Synthèse" — Analysis: prompting, auto tools, mind maps
3. "Le Créateur Multimédia" — Production: audio, video, export

Each module has 3 parts. Each part has activities and prompts.
Data model must reflect: Module → Part → Activity/Prompt

## TESTING

- Every utility: corresponding .test.ts
- Every API route: integration tests (success + validation error + auth error)
- Descriptive test names in French context
- Test edge cases: empty inputs, invalid data, unauthorized access

## DOCUMENTATION

- README.md: always reflects current project state
- docs/ARCHITECTURE.md: folder structure + design decisions
- docs/API.md: every endpoint documented
- JSDoc on every exported function
- `// WHY:` comments for non-obvious choices
- CHANGELOG.md updated for every meaningful change

## GIT CONVENTIONS

- Conventional commits: feat:, fix:, docs:, refactor:, test:, chore:, style:
- One logical change per commit
- Never commit: .env.local, node_modules/, .next/, build artifacts
- Branch naming: feature/xxx, fix/xxx, docs/xxx

## WHEN GENERATING CODE, ALWAYS:
1. Create separate files for separate concerns
2. Add TypeScript types for ALL data structures
3. Include error handling with user-friendly French messages
4. Add JSDoc comments
5. Create or update tests
6. Update documentation if behavior changes
7. Follow existing patterns
8. Use existing utils — check before creating new ones
9. Respect the design system (colors, spacing, typography)
10. All user-facing text in FRENCH

## WHEN I ASK FOR A NEW FEATURE:
1. Explain approach: files to create/modify and why
2. Wait for approval before coding
3. Create files one by one, smallest dependencies first
4. Show file structure being created
5. Add tests alongside code
6. Preview visual result when applicable

## WHEN FIXING A BUG:
1. Identify root cause
2. Write failing test
3. Fix the bug
4. Verify test passes
5. Check for same pattern elsewhere

## RESPONSE FORMAT
- Concise. Don't repeat my question
- Proper syntax highlighting
- Clearly label each file
- If unclear, ask ONE targeted question
